@page "/privacy"
@layout LegalLayout
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>Privacy Policy — Akinai</PageTitle>

<h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
<p class="lgupdated">Last updated @LastUpdated</p>

<p class="lgintro">
    Akinai is invoicing software for people who bill clients across borders. This
    page explains what we hold, why we hold it, and who else sees it. It covers
    two different sets of people: <strong>you</strong>, the account holder, and
    <strong>your clients</strong>, whose details you enter so you can invoice them.
</p>

<div class="lgnote">
    <p>
        <strong>Your clients' data belongs to you, not us.</strong> You decide what
        to record about them and why; we only store and process it so the service
        works. In UK and EU data-protection terms you are the <em>controller</em>
        and we are your <em>processor</em>. If one of your clients wants their
        details corrected or removed, they should ask you — and you can do it from
        inside the app.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Who we are</h2>
<p>
    Akinai is operated by CGH Web. For anything about this policy, or to exercise
    any of the rights below, email <a href="mailto:@ContactEmail">@ContactEmail</a>
    or use the <a href="contact">contact page</a>.
</p>

<h2>What we collect</h2>

<div class="lgtable-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>What</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Account details</strong> — email address, password (stored only as a hash), and any two-factor or passkey credentials you set up</td>
                <td>To create your account, sign you in, and keep it secure</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Business profile</strong> — company and trading name, address, tax and registration numbers, logo, contact details, and the payment details you want shown on invoices (bank account, IBAN, SWIFT, PayPal, Wise, Revolut)</td>
                <td>These appear on the invoices you send, so your clients know who is billing them and how to pay</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Client records</strong> — the names, companies, email addresses, phone numbers and billing addresses you enter</td>
                <td>To address and deliver the documents you create</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Your work</strong> — invoices, estimates, line items, notes and terms, projects, jobs, timesheets, expenses and products</td>
                <td>This is the service. It is what you came here to create</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Imported bank lines</strong> — transactions from CSV statements you choose to upload</td>
                <td>To match incoming payments against your open invoices. We never connect to your bank; import is manual and initiated by you</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Document view records</strong> — when a public invoice link was first and last opened, and how many times</td>
                <td>So you can tell whether a client has seen an invoice. We record the timestamp and count only — not who opened it, their IP address, or their location</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Billing records</strong> — your plan, subscription status, renewal date, and the identifiers Stripe gives us for your customer and subscription</td>
                <td>To run your subscription and keep an accurate history of plan changes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Translation usage</strong> — a running count of characters translated, and a cache of translated phrases</td>
                <td>To apply the monthly allowance on your plan, and to avoid paying to translate the same sentence twice</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<h3>What we do not collect</h3>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Card numbers.</strong> Payment details are entered on Stripe's own checkout page and never reach our servers. We only ever see the identifiers Stripe returns.</li>
    <li><strong>Bank credentials.</strong> There is no open-banking connection. Statement import is a file you upload.</li>
    <li><strong>Advertising or tracking profiles.</strong> We do not sell data, we do not share it with advertisers, and we run no third-party advertising or analytics trackers.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Who else processes it</h2>
<p>
    We use a small number of services to run Akinai. Each receives only what that
    job requires.
</p>

<div class="lgtable-wrap">
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Service</th><th>What it receives</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Stripe</strong><br />Payments</td>
                <td>Your email address and payment details, entered directly on Stripe's checkout. They act as their own controller for payment data under their privacy policy.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Microsoft Azure Translator</strong><br />Document translation</td>
                <td>Only the freeform text on a document you are translating — job descriptions, notes and terms. Names, addresses and payment details are never sent. See the note below.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>ExchangeRate-API</strong><br />Currency conversion</td>
                <td>Currency codes only. No personal data and no invoice amounts.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Our email provider</strong><br />Sending mail</td>
                <td>The recipient address and the contents of the message — for example an invoice you send to a client, or an account email to you.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<div class="lgnote">
    <p>
        <strong>About translation.</strong> When you send a document in a language
        other than English, the text <em>you wrote</em> on it is sent to Microsoft
        to be translated. The document's fixed headings are not — those are built
        into Akinai and cost nothing to render. If a job description or a note
        contains something you would rather not send to a third party, write it in
        the client's language yourself, or leave that document in English.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Where it is held, and for how long</h2>
<p>
    Your data is stored in our hosting provider's data centres and is transferred
    internationally where a service above requires it. We keep your account data
    for as long as your account exists.
</p>
<p>
    If you cancel a paid plan, nothing is deleted — your account simply moves to
    the Free plan and your records stay where they are. If you delete your account,
    we remove your personal data and your business records. We may retain billing
    and transaction records where we are legally required to, typically for
    accounting and tax purposes.
</p>

<h2>Your rights</h2>
<p>
    Depending on where you live, you may have the right to access your data,
    correct it, delete it, restrict or object to how it is used, and receive a
    portable copy. Two of these are built into the app and need no request:
</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Download a copy</strong> — Account → Personal data → Download</li>
    <li><strong>Delete your account and data</strong> — Account → Personal data → Delete</li>
</ul>
<p>
    For anything else, email <a href="mailto:@ContactEmail">@ContactEmail</a>. If you
    are in the UK or EU and think we have handled your data badly, you may also
    complain to your national data-protection authority.
</p>

<h2>Cookies</h2>
<p>
    Akinai sets cookies that are necessary for the service to function — keeping
    you signed in, and remembering your choices such as light or dark mode. There
    are no advertising or third-party tracking cookies, which is why you are not
    being asked to accept anything.
</p>

<h2>Security</h2>
<p>
    Passwords are stored only as salted hashes and are never recoverable in plain
    text. Two-factor authentication and passkeys are available on every account and
    we recommend enabling one of them. Access to your data is scoped to your
    account throughout the application.
</p>
<p>
    Note that invoice and estimate links you share are secured by an unguessable
    token in the URL rather than a password — that is what lets a client open one
    without an account. Anyone you send that link to can open the document, so
    treat it as you would the invoice itself.
</p>

<h2>Children</h2>
<p>
    Akinai is a business tool and is not intended for anyone under 16. We do not
    knowingly collect data from children.
</p>

<h2>Changes</h2>
<p>
    If we change this policy in a way that materially affects you, we will tell you
    by email or in the app before it takes effect. The date at the top always shows
    the current version.
</p>

@code {
    // Constants rather than DateTime.Now: the "last updated" date is a statement
    // about the document, not about when the page was rendered. Bump it by hand
    // when the wording changes.
    private const string LastUpdated = "24 July 2026";
    private const string ContactEmail = "hello@akinaiapp.com";
}
